---
title: the watchmaker
date: 2026-10-03
layout: poem
---
A watchmaker watched as she grew into everything he’d ever wanted. She had gold-flaked eyes that mimicked his, that same gold he’d fashioned into his creations, and her eyebrow raised whenever he expected. He looked at the perfect creation—*his* perfect creation, made of his blood and, unable to avoid it, his bile. Though now fully grown, her mind still had a way to go; right now, she looked up at him as if he were perfect, and that is just how he always assured it. 
Later, he smiled and spoke of his normal day-to-day ramblings, but what differed now was the response. Why are his words no longer admired? Why does he no longer gain reverence? What has shifted, drifting her away? She looks the same but something within her is changed. Her insides have been corrupted; there must be some jam, some unsolved problem to be rewound. He was an expert of mechanics—upon seeing a malfunction, he knew exactly what needed the aid of a tiny screwdriver and his magnified glasses. 
“’tis a simple fix,” he thought to himself.   
When he was done thinking, she had left the room. He was alone with his lonesomeness; and he knew that when alone, his thoughts could stretch and bend in all types of ways. When alone, he found the bottle increasingly like a companion. If anger overtook his mind, he’d pair some other depressant and use whatever was left for inspiration. Watches to be made—important work he must do, and a man to work should be left to work.   
At the moment that he could next prod her, she snapped. What was the issue, as he only wanted to rewind?   
“You can’t do this anymore.” She spoke with gravity, though her voice shaky.   
He told her he knows every now and again clocks must be adjusted; and she, made of not much more than gears turning in just the right way, required maintenance all the same. At this she stewed and stormed away, and he thought, he’s dealt with greater challenges than this.   
But no challenge matched that which revealed there’d be no more tuning. He’d been asked over to the candle-lit room, and she there--sprawled out flat--had stopped ticking. Her cheeks bloodless, suddenly he didn’t care anymore about the bile. He’d take the bile any moment of any day if it meant she would get back the blood. *His* blood now draining from his face as he understood this would be the end of his bloodline.   

